\b\fs28 POLICE STATION COMPLAINT MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\par
\b0\fs24 LEARNING ROADMAP\par
\par
Prepared by: [Your Name]\par
Date: [Insert Date]\par
\par
\b 1. Introduction\b0\par
This roadmap is designed to help you learn the skills needed to build a police station complaint management system. The system will allow staff to record complaints in text form, generate a unique report number, upload and store related photos, and create formal complaint reports for future use.\par
\par
\b 2. Goal\b0\par
The goal is to learn the technologies and skills required to build a digital complaint management system that:\par
- Records complaints in text\par
- Automatically creates a unique report number\par
- Accepts photos and stores them securely\par
- Organizes complaint data in a database\par
- Allows staff to search and manage records\par
- Produces formal reports for police use\par
\par
\b 3. Main Skills You Need to Learn\b0\par
To build this system, you need to learn the following areas:\par
- Web development basics\par
- Frontend development\par
- Backend development\par
- Database design\par
- File upload handling\par
- Report generation\par
- Security and authentication\par
- Deployment and hosting\par
- Testing and debugging\par
- Version control\par
\par
\b 4. Learning Roadmap\b0\par
\par
\b 4.1 Phase 1: Learn the Basics of Web Development\b0\par
Objective:\par
Understand how websites and web applications work.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- HTML\par
- CSS\par
- JavaScript\par
- How web pages communicate with a server\par
- Forms and input handling\par
- Basic project structure\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
This is the foundation for both the frontend and backend of the system.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- MDN Web Docs\par
- freeCodeCamp\par
- JavaScript.info\par
- YouTube beginner web development tutorials\par
\par
Time Required:\par
2 to 4 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.2 Phase 2: Learn Frontend Development\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to build the user interface where staff will:\par
- Enter complaint details\par
- Upload photos\par
- View complaints\par
- Generate reports\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- React.js\par
- Components\par
- Props and state\par
- Form handling\par
- File upload forms\par
- Responsive design\par
- Routing between pages\par
- UI libraries such as Bootstrap or Material UI\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
This is where the complaint form and dashboard will be built.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- Official React documentation\par
- freeCodeCamp React tutorials\par
- Scrimba\par
- Traversy Media tutorials\par
\par
Time Required:\par
4 to 6 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.3 Phase 3: Learn Backend Development\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to create the server that receives complaint data and stores it.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- Node.js\par
- Express.js\par
- REST APIs\par
- HTTP methods\par
- Request and response handling\par
- Validation\par
- Error handling\par
- Middleware\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
The backend is responsible for saving complaint data, generating report numbers, and handling photo uploads.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- Node.js official docs\par
- Express.js guide\par
- freeCodeCamp backend tutorials\par
- YouTube Node.js tutorials\par
\par
Time Required:\par
4 to 6 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.4 Phase 4: Learn Database Management\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to store complaint data in a structured and reliable way.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- SQL basics\par
- PostgreSQL\par
- Tables and relationships\par
- Primary keys and foreign keys\par
- Insert, update, delete, and select\par
- Filters and search queries\par
- Database design for complaint records\par
\par
Important database tables:\par
- Complaints\par
- Users\par
- Complaint statuses\par
- Evidence/photos\par
- Reports\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
A complaint system needs organized storage for records and evidence.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- PostgreSQL official documentation\par
- W3Schools SQL\par
- freeCodeCamp SQL tutorials\par
\par
Time Required:\par
2 to 4 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.5 Phase 5: Learn File Upload and Photo Storage\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to upload complaint-related images and store them safely.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- File uploads in web apps\par
- Image validation\par
- File size limits\par
- File type restrictions\par
- Saving uploaded files to the server\par
- Organizing files by report number\par
- Optional cloud storage with AWS S3 or Google Cloud Storage\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
This is essential because the system must keep complaint evidence and photos for future use.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- Express file upload tutorials\par
- Multer library\par
- AWS S3 documentation (optional)\par
\par
Time Required:\par
1 to 2 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.6 Phase 6: Learn Report Generation\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to generate complaint reports in a professional format.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- PDF generation\par
- Report templates\par
- Formatting complaint content\par
- Adding images to generated reports\par
- Exporting to PDF\par
\par
Recommended Tools:\par
- PDFKit\par
- jsPDF\par
- Puppeteer\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
The system should generate a formal report for each complaint.\par
\par
Time Required:\par
1 to 2 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.7 Phase 7: Learn Authentication and Security\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to protect the complaint system from unauthorized access.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- User login\par
- Password hashing\par
- JWT or session-based authentication\par
- Role-based access control\par
- Secure API design\par
- HTTPS\par
- Data privacy basics\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
Only authorized staff should be able to access records and photos.\par
\par
Recommended Resources:\par
- JWT documentation\par
- Passport.js tutorials\par
- Node.js authentication tutorials\par
\par
Time Required:\par
2 to 3 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.8 Phase 8: Learn Testing and Debugging\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to verify that the system works correctly and fix bugs.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- Unit testing\par
- Integration testing\par
- Debugging tools\par
- Error handling\par
- Common bug fixing techniques\par
- Testing APIs and forms\par
\par
Recommended Tools:\par
- Jest\par
- Postman\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
This helps ensure that the complaint system works reliably.\par
\par
Time Required:\par
2 weeks\par
\par
\b 4.9 Phase 9: Learn Git and GitHub\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to manage your code and collaborate.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- Git basics\par
- Commit, push, pull, merge\par
- Branching\par
- GitHub repositories\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
This is essential for tracking the project and saving versions of your work.\par
\par
Time Required:\par
1 week\par
\par
\b 4.10 Phase 10: Learn Deployment\b0\par
Objective:\par
Learn how to make the app available online for real use.\par
\par
Topics to Learn:\par
- Hosting platforms\par
- Environment variables\par
- Database hosting\par
- Server setup\par
- Deployment workflows\par
- Domain configuration\par
\par
Recommended Platforms:\par
- Heroku\par
- DigitalOcean\par
- Vercel\par
- AWS\par
\par
Why it matters:\par
After building the system, you need to deploy it so staff can use it.\par
\par
Time Required:\par
1 to 2 weeks\par
\par
\b 5. Minimum Skills Required Before Building the System\b0\par
Before you begin building the full system, you should be comfortable with:\par
- JavaScript basics\par
- React.js\par
- Node.js and Express.js\par
- Basic SQL and PostgreSQL\par
- CRUD operations\par
- File uploads\par
- Git and GitHub\par
- Deployment basics\par
\par
\b 6. Suggested Learning Order\b0\par
The best order is:\par
1. HTML, CSS, JavaScript\par
2. React.js\par
3. Node.js and Express.js\par
4. PostgreSQL and SQL\par
5. File upload handling\par
6. Report generation\par
7. Authentication\par
8. Testing\par
9. Deployment\par
\par
This order helps you build the system in a logical way.\par
\par
\b 7. Practical Projects to Build While Learning\b0\par
To strengthen your understanding, build small projects such as:\par
- Student record system\par
- Inventory management app\par
- Complaint form app\par
- Photo gallery app\par
- Task management system\par
- Simple report generator\par
\par
These projects help you practice the same concepts used in the police complaint system.\par
\par
\b 8. Recommended Time Plan\b0\par
A realistic schedule could be:\par
\par
Month 1:\par
- HTML, CSS, JavaScript\par
- Basic frontend concepts\par
\par
Month 2:\par
- React.js\par
- Form handling\par
- UI design\par
\par
Month 3:\par
- Node.js\par
- Express.js\par
- APIs\par
\par
Month 4:\par
- PostgreSQL\par
- Database design\par
- File upload handling\par
\par
Month 5:\par
- Report generation\par
- Authentication\par
- Testing\par
\par
Month 6:\par
- Deployment\par
- Bug fixing\par
- Final project integration\par
\par
\b 9. Tools You Need to Install\b0\par
Before starting, install the following:\par
- VS Code\par
- Node.js\par
- PostgreSQL\par
- Postman\par
- Git\par
- Browser (Google Chrome or Edge)\par
- NPM package manager\par
\par
\b 10. Final Advice\b0\par
Do not try to build the full system immediately. Start with small projects and learn step by step. First, build a simple complaint form. Then add the report number. Then add image upload. Then connect the database. After that, add security and reporting. This makes the process easier and more manageable.\par
\par
\b 11. Conclusion\b0\par
To build a police station complaint management system, you need to learn web development, frontend design, backend logic, database management, file handling, report generation, security, and deployment. This roadmap gives you a clear path from beginner to working project.\par
\par
By following the steps in this roadmap, you will gradually build the skills and confidence needed to create the system successfully.\par
\par
